<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the proposed Consistent View Alignment (CVA) framework. Two random crops from the same image are produced such that they have an overlapping region (shaded blue) covering 40% to 80% of the image, ensuring a region of consistency between crops. They are then encoded by student and teacher networks (teacher updated via EMA). The reconstruction branch follows a masked autoencoding objective similar to standard reconstruction-based approaches, while the alignment branch uses bounding boxes of the overlapping regions to produce aligned feature maps via ROIAlign. A consistency loss enforces local feature agreement only on matched regions, mitigating SimCLR-like spurious alignments and regularising the latent space structure to yield consistent features across varying spatial contexts, pixel-level details, and semantic content.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote an input image or volume. We generate two random crops <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2}=\mathcal{T}_{1}(\mathbf{x}),\mathcal{T}_{2}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2}=\mathcal{T}_{1}(\mathbf{x}),\mathcal{T}_{2}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> via data augmentations <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1},\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1},\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from a family <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. In conventional contrastive learning, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},v_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},v_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are treated as a positive pair, assumed to represent the same underlying semantics. This assumption may not hold when crops cause a view mismatch or when the image contains heterogeneous content. Our approach mitigates this by sampling crops with a shared region, ensuring a region of consistency between crops.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dual-Branch Architecture.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a student-teacher architecture, where the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">student encoder</span> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained via gradient descent, and the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">teacher encoder</span> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated with an exponential moving average (EMA) of the student parameters:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\leftarrow\tau\xi+(1-\tau)\theta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\leftarrow\tau\xi+(1-\tau)\theta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> controls the EMA decay rate. <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> can be set to a non-zero value similar to BYOL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, or the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> can be set to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, like in SimSiam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, where the teacher and student are always the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given crops <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{1}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{1}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{\theta}(v_{1}),\quad\mathbf{z}_{2}^{s}=f_{\theta}(v_{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{\theta}(v_{1}),\quad\mathbf{z}_{2}^{s}=f_{\theta}(v_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{1}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{1}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{\xi}(v_{1}),\quad\mathbf{z}_{2}^{t}=f_{\xi}(v_{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{\xi}(v_{1}),\quad\mathbf{z}_{2}^{t}=f_{\xi}(v_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tilde{C}\times\tilde{H}\times\tilde{W}\times\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tilde{C}\times\tilde{H}\times\tilde{W}\times\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are spatial feature maps.
Generally, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}\gg C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≫</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}\gg C</annotation></semantics></math> and the spatial dimensions are smaller.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Projector-Predictor Setup.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following standard practice in contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, the spatially pooled latent features <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> from the encoder are passed through a non-linear <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projector</span> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, implemented as a two- or three-layer MLP:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}=p_{\theta}(\mathbf{z})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}=p_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For the student branch, the setup further appends a <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predictor</span> <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps the projected features to the prediction space:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=q_{\theta}(\mathbf{h})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=q_{\theta}(\mathbf{h}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is compared to a target feature from another view, typically obtained from a (teacher) momentum encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> or a stop-gradient branch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The intuition behind the predictor is to “digest” information, while the projector attempts to disentangle information. This predictor-projector design has been shown to stabilise training and improve feature representation in self-distillation settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contrastive Loss with Symmetrisation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The output of the projector-predictor can then be used in a contrastive learning objective similar to SimCLR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, CMAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, with symmetrization like in swapped assignment from SwAV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the predictor outputs for two augmented views, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{t}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{t}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding projected features from the target branch. The SimCLR loss can be rewritten as Normalized Temperature-scaled Cross Entropy (NT-Xent) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and in one direction it is:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2})=-\log\frac{\exp\left(\mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2})/\tau\right)}{\sum\limits_{k=1}^{2N}\mathbbm{1}_{[k\neq i]}\exp\left(\mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{k})/\tau\right)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>NT-Xent</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>sim</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2})=-\log\frac{\exp\left(\mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2})/\tau\right)}{\sum\limits_{k=1}^{2N}\mathbbm{1}_{[k\neq i]}\exp\left(\mathrm{sim}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{k})/\tau\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sim}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sim}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes cosine similarity, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of original samples in the batch (resulting in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> augmented examples). The indicator <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbbm{1}_{[k\neq i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}_{[k\neq i]}</annotation></semantics></math> masks out comparisons with the same view. This objective is <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetrised</span> by computing the loss in both directions and averaging:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}^{\text{sym}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2})+\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}(\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>NT-Xent</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>NT-Xent</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>NT-Xent</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}^{\text{sym}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}(\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{2})+\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{NT-Xent}}(\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{h}^{t}_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In the SwAV formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the same principle applies in the assignment space: let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{1},\mathbf{p}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{1},\mathbf{p}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be probability vectors over <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> clusters obtained via Sinkhorn-Knopp normalisation, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{1},\mathbf{q}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{1},\mathbf{q}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the predicted assignments. The swapped prediction loss is:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SwAV}}=-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbf{p}_{2,k}\log\mathbf{q}_{1,k}-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbf{p}_{1,k}\log\mathbf{q}_{2,k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>SwAV</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>𝐩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝐪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>𝐩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>𝐪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SwAV}}=-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbf{p}_{2,k}\log\mathbf{q}_{1,k}-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbf{p}_{1,k}\log\mathbf{q}_{2,k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Symmetrisation has been shown to be effective in mitigating training instability and improving robustness to view and label noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Masked Autoencoding Reconstruction Loss.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To encourage global semantic encoding, CMAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> introduces a masked reconstruction objective paired with a contrastive term, where the method randomly masks a high proportion <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of the volume/tokens <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">only</span> for the student and reconstructs the missing patches via a lightweight decoder <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}}l(g_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{1}^{s})_{i},v_{1,i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>recon</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}}l(g_{\theta}(\mathbf{z}_{1}^{s})_{i},v_{1,i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> indexes masked positions and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is a specified distance function either <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\delta}_{\text{huber}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mtext>huber</mtext><mi>δ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\delta}_{\text{huber}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{huber}}^{\delta}(y,\hat{y})=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}(y-\hat{y})^{2}&amp;\text{if }|y-\hat{y}|\leq\delta\\
\delta\cdot\left(|y-\hat{y}|-\frac{1}{2}\delta\right)&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mtext>huber</mtext><mi>δ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{huber}}^{\delta}(y,\hat{y})=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}(y-\hat{y})^{2}&amp;\text{if }|y-\hat{y}|\leq\delta\\
\delta\cdot\left(|y-\hat{y}|-\frac{1}{2}\delta\right)&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}(y,\hat{y})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}-\hat{y}_{i})^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}(y,\hat{y})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}-\hat{y}_{i})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Consistent View Alignment (CVA)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The central novelty of CVA lies in an <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alignment step</span> that leverages spatial correspondences between two augmented views <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to prevent false-positive alignments. Specifically, during the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consistent view generation</span> step, we record the coordinates of the overlapping region between the two crops. This overlap is later used to spatially align feature maps before applying a consistency loss, ensuring that only semantically corresponding regions are compared.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Consistent View Generation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an original image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate two crops <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2}=\mathcal{T}_{1}(\mathbf{x}),\mathcal{T}_{2}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2}=\mathcal{T}_{1}(\mathbf{x}),\mathcal{T}_{2}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> with a fixed output patch volume of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> using random spatial sampling with a constraint on overlap:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\text{min}}\leq\frac{\mathrm{Area}(\mathrm{Overlap}(v_{1},v_{2}))}{V_{p}}\leq\gamma_{\text{max}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Overlap</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\text{min}}\leq\frac{\mathrm{Area}(\mathrm{Overlap}(v_{1},v_{2}))}{V_{p}}\leq\gamma_{\text{max}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\text{min}}=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\text{min}}=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\text{max}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\text{max}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to volume of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>160</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments. The resulting bounding boxes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the relative overlap location for each crop are stored for later use in feature alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feature Maps.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For convolutional backbones, we follow VoCo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. We extract feature maps from multiple stages, resize each to a fixed spatial resolution via bilinear interpolation, and concatenate them along the channel dimension:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{F}=\mathrm{Concat}\big[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Concat</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{F}=\mathrm{Concat}\big[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Resize}(\mathbf{F}_{1}),\dots,\mathrm{Resize}(\mathbf{F}_{S})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Resize</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Resize</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Resize}(\mathbf{F}_{1}),\dots,\mathrm{Resize}(\mathbf{F}_{S})\big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}\times D^{\prime}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C^{\prime}\times H^{\prime}\times W^{\prime}\times D^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For transformer-based backbones we take the final encoder output before the classification head and perform the inverse of the tokenisation step to produce a spatial feature map similar to the convolution case.
When combined with a masked reconstruction objective, we follow Contrastive MAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and introduce a separate <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">feature decoder</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pixel decoder</span> to predict the masked regions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feature Consistency.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the alignment step for reinforcing feature consistency, the feature maps are passed through the projector-predictor modules and then use RoiAlign (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ROI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ROI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ROI}</annotation></semantics></math>) using respective <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> to produce aligned feature maps of the same resolution:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h^{\Omega}}=\operatorname{ROI}(p_{\theta}(\mathbf{F}),\Omega),\quad\mathbf{u^{\Omega}}=\operatorname{ROI}(q_{\theta}(\mathbf{h}),\Omega)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝛀</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ROI</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>𝛀</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ROI</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h^{\Omega}}=\operatorname{ROI}(p_{\theta}(\mathbf{F}),\Omega),\quad\mathbf{u^{\Omega}}=\operatorname{ROI}(q_{\theta}(\mathbf{h}),\Omega),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-linear projector and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> a predictor network as introduced before.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that our feature maps are volumetric; therefore, we flatten the spatial dimensions after the alignment step. Now we can adopt the cosine regression loss from SimSiam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, which does not require negative examples:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cos}}(\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2})=2-2\frac{\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1}\cdot\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2}}{\|\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1}\|_{2}\;\|\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2}\|_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cos</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cos}}(\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2})=2-2\frac{\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1}\cdot\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2}}{\|\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1}\|_{2}\;\|\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2}\|_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We further adopt <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetrisation</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> to compute the loss in both view directions:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{cos}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cos}}(\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2})+\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cos}}(\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{2}}_{2},\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{1}},t}_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cos</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cos</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cos</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{cos}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cos}}(\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{1}}_{1},\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{2}},t}_{2})+\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cos}}(\mathbf{u}^{\Omega_{2}}_{2},\mathbf{h}^{{\Omega_{1}},t}_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Overall Objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The overall training loss is a weighted combination of reconstruction, consistency, and optionally contrastive objectives:</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{\text{recon}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}}+\lambda_{\text{consis}}\,\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{consis}}+\lambda_{\text{con}}\,\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{con}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>recon</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>recon</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>consis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>consis</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>con</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{\text{recon}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}}+\lambda_{\text{consis}}\,\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{consis}}+\lambda_{\text{con}}\,\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{con}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{recon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>recon</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{recon}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{consis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>consis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{consis}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{con}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{con}}</annotation></semantics></math> are balancing hyperparameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The reconstruction loss <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>recon</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{recon}}</annotation></semantics></math> follows standard masked autoencoding loss, here it is fixed to the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{huber}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>huber</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{huber}}</annotation></semantics></math> loss. The consistency loss <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{consis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>consis</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{consis}}</annotation></semantics></math> enforces alignment between semantically corresponding regions, thereby reducing the impact of false positives and improving robustness. For this term, we can use either the symmetrised cosine regression loss (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{cos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cos</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{cos}}</annotation></semantics></math>) or the symmetrised NT-Xent variant (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{NT-Xent}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>NT-Xent</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{NT-Xent}}</annotation></semantics></math>), referred to as CVA and C-CVA respectively. Finally, the optional contrastive term <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{con}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>con</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sym}}_{\text{con}}</annotation></semantics></math> further regularises the latent space to encourage transferable representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, the superscript <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">sym</span> indicates that we compute the losses in both view directions, following the symmetrisation scheme described in the previous section.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments and Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pre-training Dataset.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We pretrain all models on the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OpenMind</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, the largest publicly available head &amp; neck MRI collection, comprising <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="114{,}570" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>114,570</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">114{,}570</annotation></semantics></math> 3D volumes from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="34{,}191" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>34,191</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">34{,}191</annotation></semantics></math> patients. The dataset aggregates over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">800</annotation></semantics></math> smaller datasets released under CC0 or PDDL licences, harmonized in image format and metadata. It includes a wide spectrum of MRI sequences such as T1-weighted (T1w), T2-weighted (T2w), FLAIR, Mean Diffusion (MD), Fractional Anisotropy (FA), and several less common modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Architectures.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that our approach is, to some extent, agnostic to the kind of deep learning architecture used. We evaluate two representative architectures: the convolutional ResEnc-L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and the transformer-based Primus-M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. This allows us to test CVA across distinct inductive biases and computational trade-offs. Note Primus-M does not have a <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cls</span> token and therefore the image latent vector is calculated by performing attention pooling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> instead of a simple average.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Augmentation Pipeline.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our augmentation strategy follows a spatial-then-color paradigm. First, spatial transformations (mirroring, scaling, rotation, simulated low resolution) are applied. Second, crop pairs with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\%</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math> overlap are generated. Finally, an intensity transformation is applied from a set of noise injections (Rician or Gaussian), Gaussian blur (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\{0.5,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\{0.5,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>), and photometric changes (brightness, contrast, gamma). While each crop can have a spatial transform independently, it complicates the alignment step, and hence this design choice was made. Validation uses the same consistent view generation but omits other transformations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Specifications.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a two-stage pretraining protocol. In <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stage one</span>, we train an MAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> baseline for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> epochs: ResEnc-L (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>5 days) and Primus-M (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>14 days) on a single NVIDIA A40 GPU. These serve as initialization for all subsequent experiments, enabling shorter training cycles and reducing compute cost, particularly for transformer models. An MAE model was chosen as it appeared to be the pareto optimal model for segmentation and classification downstream tasks in 3D Brain Segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. We fix the use of the reconstruction loss, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the Huber reconstruction loss: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\delta=1}_{\text{huber}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mtext>huber</mtext><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\delta=1}_{\text{huber}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stage two</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">post-pretraining</span>), we train all models with their respective objectives: ResEnc-L for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>250</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">250</annotation></semantics></math> epochs (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>25 h, initial LR=<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.005</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.005</annotation></semantics></math>), and Primus-M for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> epochs (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>48 h, initial LR=<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0003</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0003</annotation></semantics></math>). We always use the final model for downstream fine-tuning tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream Fine-tuning.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on both segmentation and classification benchmarks:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Segmentation:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">YBM</span> - Yale Brain Metastasis: <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">enhancing tumor</span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-enhancing tumor</span>, and <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">oedema</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GLI</span> - BraTS Post-Glioblastoma Recurrence: <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">enhancing tumor</span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">necrotic core</span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">oedema</span>, and <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-enhancing tumor</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ISL</span> - ISLES 2022 Ischemic Stroke Lesions: <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stroke lesion</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MSD</span> - Medical Segmentation Decathlon Brain Tumor Segmentation: <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whole tumor</span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tumor core</span>, and <span id="S4.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">enhancing tumor</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For all segmentation datasets, we use a <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> train-validation split, and report the mean of the average Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC) and Normalized Surface Dice (NSD, 1 mm threshold) computed per class.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classification:</span>
<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ABD II</span> - ABIDE-II (Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange II), MPRAGE T1-weighted channel only: binary classification between <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)</span> and <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">healthy control</span>. We report balanced accuracy, AUROC, and average precision, averaged over three-fold splits.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All fine-tuning runs last <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math> epochs with configurations detailed in our code repository.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines and Ablations</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our CVA method against: (1) MAE-trained baselines without post-pretraining, and (2) Contrastive MAE. Ablations investigate the effect of different consistency loss formulations and the addition of a contrastive term from Contrastive MAE in our framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Comparison of segmentation and classification performance across ResEnc-L and Primus-M tracks under different reconstruction, consistency, and contrastive configurations.
The <span id="S4.T1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recon.</span> column indicates the reconstruction strategy: <span id="S4.T1.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MAE</span> denotes Masked Autoencoder reconstruction, while <span id="S4.T1.12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AE</span> corresponds to a standard Autoencoder without masking.
The <span id="S4.T1.12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consis.</span> column specifies the consistency regularization: a 
<span id="S4.T1.12.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:8.1pt;height:9.800000000000001pt;vertical-align:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.7pt,-0.8pt) scale(1.2,1.2) ;">
<span id="S4.T1.12.5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T1.12.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></span>
</span></span> indicates no consistency loss; <span id="S4.T1.12.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CVA</span> refers to the objective using cosine similarity; <span id="S4.T1.12.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C-CVA</span> refers to the NT-Xent contrastive consistency objective.
The <span id="S4.T1.12.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cont.</span> column indicates whether a global contrastive term is used, in addition to reconstruction and consistency losses.
Performance is reported for four segmentation datasets and one classification task. Lower ranks indicate better performance.
</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.13" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:141.6pt;vertical-align:-69.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-160.1pt,45.6pt) scale(0.60806366489384,0.60806366489384) ;">
