<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the new ranking method shown in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6 Alternate Rankings ‣ Consistent View Alignment Improves Foundation Models for 3D Medical Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we see that in the average rankings Contrastive MAE is much closer to the CVA with the same contrastive term for ResEnc-L and for Primus-M it actually surpasses CVA with the contrastive term by large margin. The relative rankings do not change for segmentation tasks. This new ranking method therefore, highlights the importance of the choice of ranking and to critically consider the existence of small dynamic range of metrics on a give task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Gram Consistency Variant</h2>

<figure id="S7.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S7.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of the Gram consistency variant across segmentation and classification benchmarks.
Values are reported as percentages. MAE was used as the reconstruction loss, and no contrastive signal was added.</span></figcaption>
