<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the flexibility of our framework, we experimented with an alternative consistency objective based on Gram matrix anchoring.
Here, local feature correlations are matched across views by minimizing the Frobenius norm between their Gram matrices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose an image is divided into <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> patches/voxels, and the network produces <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional features.
Let <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1}^{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1}^{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> (student) and
<math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{2}^{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{2}^{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> (teacher) denote the <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized patch features for two crops of the same volume.
These features are taken directly after our alignment step (we omit <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> in superscript for clarity) and without a projector-predictor module.
The Gram anchoring loss is defined as</p>
<table id="S7.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}=\big\|z_{1}^{s}{z_{1}^{s}}^{\top}-z_{2}^{t}{z_{2}^{t}}^{\top}\big\|_{F}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Gram</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}=\big\|z_{1}^{s}{z_{1}^{s}}^{\top}-z_{2}^{t}{z_{2}^{t}}^{\top}\big\|_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and can be symmetrized as</p>
<table id="S7.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}^{\text{sym}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}(z_{1}^{s},z_{2}^{t})+\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}(z_{2}^{s},z_{1}^{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Gram</mtext><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Gram</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Gram</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}^{\text{sym}}=\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}(z_{1}^{s},z_{2}^{t})+\frac{1}{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Gram}}(z_{2}^{s},z_{1}^{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to cosine regression, which operates in <em id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">first-order space</em> by aligning features directly,
Gram anchoring acts in <em id="S7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">second-order space</em> by aligning the pairwise similarity structure between features.
First-order alignment enforces that corresponding feature vectors point in similar directions, whereas second-order alignment allows features to move freely as long as their relative correlations are preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7 Gram Consistency Variant ‣ Consistent View Alignment Improves Foundation Models for 3D Medical Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the results. All models were trained with MAE reconstruction and no contrastive signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, Gram consistency appears beneficial for ResEnc-L, with consistent improvements across segmentation datasets.
For Primus-M, however, the gains are inconsistent, suggesting that transformer-based models may benefit less from this
form of second-order alignment in the medical domain. Notably, classification performance on ABIDE II decreases substantially in both
architectures, indicating a trade-off that requires further investigation. In future work, we plan to ablate
whether combining Gram consistency with a contrastive signal can recover classification performance, similar to
our findings with cosine regression and NT-Xent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
